   "family": "gjuhë",
   "is_root": true,
   "etymology": "Unclear. From Old Albanian gluhë, most likely from earlier *glu.a, with sporadic loss of intervocalic b (see pi, det, and dra) and insertion of a hiatus-filling h (see bahe, kohë, and pah), from Proto-Albanian *glubā (“tip”), from Proto-Indo-European *ǵlubʰeh₂- (“point”), albeit with no adduced cognates. Alternatively, from a Proto-Albanian *glusā, from Proto-Indo-European *gl̥H-so-s, a zero-grade variant of *golH-so-s, from *gelH- (“to call”); compare Proto-Balto-Slavic *galsás (“voice”).\nA loan from Ancient Greek γλῶσσα (glôssa) is improbable from a semantic and phonetic standpoint, as Ancient Greek loans involved terms of trade and agriculture, not body parts, and would have given **gluśa in Proto-Albanian.",
   "etymology_class": "inherited",
